1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,747
She isn't his wife, and
she has just realised it.

2
00:00:04,617 --> 00:00:07,877
At first glance, a young
couple at the piano.

3
00:00:07,897 --> 00:00:09,717
Then you see her hands.

4
00:00:09,737 --> 00:00:13,415
A ring on almost every finger,
except the one for a wedding.

5
00:00:14,155 --> 00:00:18,295
The man who painted her believed a
woman like this could still be saved.

6
00:00:18,315 --> 00:00:21,415
And in real life, he
tried to remake his model:

7
00:00:21,435 --> 00:00:23,870
a barmaid from the back
streets of Chelsea.

8
00:00:24,610 --> 00:00:26,590
It didn't go the way he planned.

9
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:30,433
Even the face you see today
isn't the face he first painted.

10
00:00:31,173 --> 00:00:34,593
This is the story of two
women: the one he painted,

11
00:00:34,613 --> 00:00:36,468
and the one who wouldn't play the part.

12
00:00:38,568 --> 00:00:43,028
In 1851, William Holman Hunt
is a young painter in London,

13
00:00:43,048 --> 00:00:45,748
one of the founders of the
Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood,

14
00:00:45,768 --> 00:00:48,450
a small band of rebels
against the Royal Academy.

15
00:00:49,190 --> 00:00:52,370
On his easel in Chelsea is a
picture of Christ at night,

16
00:00:52,390 --> 00:00:55,410
knocking at a door overgrown with weeds.

17
00:00:55,430 --> 00:00:57,561
He calls it The Light of the World.

18
00:00:58,301 --> 00:01:00,601
And he starts to think about its opposite.

19
00:01:00,621 --> 00:01:03,561
Not a vision, but a
scene from modern life:

20
00:01:03,581 --> 00:01:06,551
a soul being called
away from a lower life.

21
00:01:07,291 --> 00:01:11,511
He had been reading David Copperfield,
and he was moved by old Peggotty,

22
00:01:11,531 --> 00:01:15,106
searching for little Emily
after she has become an outcast.

23
00:01:15,586 --> 00:01:21,006
So Hunt went around, in his own words,
to the different haunts of fallen girls,

24
00:01:21,026 --> 00:01:22,312
looking for a setting.

25
00:01:23,052 --> 00:01:25,832
Then he found a verse
in the Book of Proverbs.

26
00:01:25,852 --> 00:01:28,792
As he that taketh away a
garment in cold weather,

27
00:01:28,812 --> 00:01:31,805
so is he that singeth
songs to an heavy heart.

28
00:01:32,805 --> 00:01:38,065
A song, sung by the wrong man at the
wrong moment, could wake a conscience.

29
00:01:38,085 --> 00:01:42,265
The man who had led her astray would
deliver the message without knowing it.

30
00:01:43,005 --> 00:01:45,385
His friend, the painter Augustus Egg,

31
00:01:45,405 --> 00:01:49,715
talked about the idea to a Manchester
industrialist, Thomas Fairbairn.

32
00:01:49,735 --> 00:01:51,865
Fairbairn commissioned the picture.

33
00:01:51,885 --> 00:01:55,348
And Hunt put off a journey
to the Holy Land to paint it.

34
00:01:57,248 --> 00:01:59,388
Hunt wanted the room to be true.

35
00:01:59,408 --> 00:02:03,708
So he rented one in St John's Wood,
at a house called Woodbine Villa.

36
00:02:03,728 --> 00:02:06,428
The Tate calls it a maison de convenance:

37
00:02:06,448 --> 00:02:09,002
a house where gentlemen
met the women they kept.

38
00:02:09,742 --> 00:02:11,402
Look at the furniture.

39
00:02:11,422 --> 00:02:12,762
Everything is new.

40
00:02:12,782 --> 00:02:16,270
The fine books on the table look
as if no one has ever read them.

41
00:02:17,010 --> 00:02:20,990
The critic John Ruskin would call
it the fatal newness of the room.

42
00:02:21,010 --> 00:02:23,162
Nothing in it had ever
been part of a home.

43
00:02:24,032 --> 00:02:26,462
Hunt worked on it through 1853.

44
00:02:26,672 --> 00:02:29,372
The dark London winter slowed him down.

45
00:02:29,392 --> 00:02:31,932
He needed one bright day to finish.

46
00:02:31,952 --> 00:02:35,132
In January 1854, it came.

47
00:02:35,152 --> 00:02:36,959
By four o'clock, he was done.

48
00:02:37,699 --> 00:02:41,999
That same night, he caught the
mail train, on his way to the East.

49
00:02:42,019 --> 00:02:45,599
His friend John Everett Millais
ran to the station buffet

50
00:02:45,619 --> 00:02:47,341
to grab him something to eat.

51
00:02:49,241 --> 00:02:51,461
But who is the woman at the piano?

52
00:02:51,481 --> 00:02:52,996
Her name was Annie Miller.

53
00:02:53,866 --> 00:02:58,086
She was born in 1835 in Chelsea,
the daughter of a soldier

54
00:02:58,106 --> 00:03:00,763
who had been wounded
in the Napoleonic Wars.

55
00:03:01,503 --> 00:03:07,163
Hunt first noticed her in 1850, when
she was fifteen, an uneducated barmaid

56
00:03:07,183 --> 00:03:09,388
at a pub near his studio.

57
00:03:10,128 --> 00:03:13,788
Hunt was a deeply religious
man, and he made a plan.

58
00:03:13,808 --> 00:03:18,748
He would pay for her to learn to read and
write, to speak and behave like a lady.

59
00:03:18,768 --> 00:03:21,363
And when she was ready,
he would marry her.

60
00:03:22,233 --> 00:03:27,253
On the canvas, he was painting a
fallen woman called to a better life.

61
00:03:27,273 --> 00:03:31,902
In his own life, he was trying to
write the same story with a real woman.

62
00:03:32,902 --> 00:03:37,042
When he left for the East, he left
her lessons, money for her keep,

63
00:03:37,062 --> 00:03:39,861
and a list of the painters
she was allowed to sit for.

64
00:03:40,601 --> 00:03:44,981
One name, the story goes, was
definitely not on that list:

65
00:03:45,001 --> 00:03:48,630
his fellow founder of the
Brotherhood, Dante Gabriel Rossetti.

66
00:03:50,530 --> 00:03:52,830
Now look closely at the painting.

67
00:03:52,850 --> 00:03:55,674
Hunt meant it to be read, like a page.

68
00:03:56,544 --> 00:03:59,404
Under the table, a cat has caught a bird.

69
00:03:59,424 --> 00:04:01,219
The bird's wing is broken.

70
00:04:02,089 --> 00:04:05,909
On the floor, a man's glove,
pulled off and dropped.

71
00:04:05,929 --> 00:04:10,229
The Tate reads it as a warning:
a kept woman, once cast off,

72
00:04:10,249 --> 00:04:12,220
was likely to end up on the street.

73
00:04:13,090 --> 00:04:18,740
Beside it, a tangle of wool from an
unfinished tapestry: the web she's caught in.

74
00:04:19,610 --> 00:04:25,093
On the piano, a clock with a small figure
of a woman, sealed under a glass dome.

75
00:04:25,963 --> 00:04:31,303
On the floor, a song sheet: Tears,
Idle Tears, Tennyson's poem,

76
00:04:31,323 --> 00:04:33,783
set to music by Edward Lear.

77
00:04:33,803 --> 00:04:38,423
And on the piano, the song he's playing:
Thomas Moore's Oft in the Stilly Night,

78
00:04:38,443 --> 00:04:41,028
a song about the memory of happier days.

79
00:04:41,898 --> 00:04:46,358
Behind them, in the mirror, you
can see what she sees: a window,

80
00:04:46,378 --> 00:04:48,820
and a garden in the spring sunshine.

81
00:04:49,690 --> 00:04:51,670
Even the frame tells the story.

82
00:04:51,690 --> 00:04:56,550
Hunt designed it himself: bells
for warning, marigolds for sorrow,

83
00:04:56,570 --> 00:04:58,742
and above her head, a star.

84
00:04:59,742 --> 00:05:03,882
Hunt had his own title for
it: The Still Small Voice.

85
00:05:03,902 --> 00:05:08,442
It was a friend in Oxford, Thomas Combe,
who suggested the one it still has:

86
00:05:08,462 --> 00:05:10,304
The Awakening Conscience.

87
00:05:12,204 --> 00:05:16,984
In the spring of 1854, the Royal Academy
showed it, in the same exhibition

88
00:05:17,004 --> 00:05:18,624
as The Light of the World.

89
00:05:19,364 --> 00:05:21,104
The critics were baffled.

90
00:05:21,124 --> 00:05:24,064
Some visitors thought they were
looking at a brother and sister,

91
00:05:24,084 --> 00:05:25,360
in the middle of a quarrel.

92
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:30,071
The Morning Chronicle called it an
absolutely disagreeable picture.

93
00:05:30,811 --> 00:05:34,391
And the Athenaeum wrote that the
author of The Bridge of Sighs,

94
00:05:34,411 --> 00:05:39,191
a poem about a woman who drowns herself
in the Thames, could not have conceived

95
00:05:39,211 --> 00:05:41,266
a more painful-looking face.

96
00:05:42,266 --> 00:05:46,566
Then, in late May, John Ruskin, the
great defender of the Brotherhood,

97
00:05:46,586 --> 00:05:48,246
wrote to The Times.

98
00:05:48,266 --> 00:05:52,801
People gaze at it in a blank wonder,
he wrote, and leave it hopelessly.

99
00:05:53,671 --> 00:05:55,091
So he explained it.

100
00:05:55,111 --> 00:05:58,131
The song, the room, the bird.

101
00:05:58,151 --> 00:06:00,851
And her face: the lips half open,

102
00:06:00,871 --> 00:06:06,497
the eyes filled with the fearful light of
futurity, and with tears of ancient days.

103
00:06:07,497 --> 00:06:12,517
There is not a single object in all that
room, he wrote, but it becomes tragical,

104
00:06:12,537 --> 00:06:13,787
if rightly read.

105
00:06:14,677 --> 00:06:19,747
Far away in the Holy Land, Hunt read
Ruskin's letter, and was encouraged.

106
00:06:19,797 --> 00:06:23,057
But in Manchester, the man
who had paid for the painting

107
00:06:23,077 --> 00:06:25,055
would have to live with that face.

108
00:06:26,955 --> 00:06:29,988
Back in London, Annie was
not following the plan.

109
00:06:30,858 --> 00:06:33,798
She sat for Millais, who was on the list.

110
00:06:33,818 --> 00:06:38,729
But she also sat for Rossetti, and for his
friend, the painter George Price Boyce.

111
00:06:39,469 --> 00:06:42,649
They took her out to restaurants,
for walks in the park,

112
00:06:42,669 --> 00:06:46,290
and to the Cremorne pleasure
gardens, where she danced with Boyce.

113
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:49,430
Hunt came home in 1856.

114
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:54,438
When he heard, he is said to have
declared: I will never forgive Gabriel.

115
00:06:55,438 --> 00:06:57,098
And there was a second problem.

116
00:06:57,118 --> 00:06:59,818
Fairbairn could not bear
the woman's expression.

117
00:06:59,838 --> 00:07:01,821
He asked Hunt to soften it.

118
00:07:02,691 --> 00:07:05,151
So Hunt worked on her face again.

119
00:07:05,171 --> 00:07:06,671
He was ill with fever.

120
00:07:06,691 --> 00:07:11,311
The picture went off to an exhibition in
Birmingham before he was happy with it.

121
00:07:11,331 --> 00:07:13,711
The next spring he tried again.

122
00:07:13,731 --> 00:07:17,851
I think that I have materially bettered
it now, he wrote to Edward Lear.

123
00:07:18,801 --> 00:07:22,061
But half a century later,
he still had his doubts.

124
00:07:22,081 --> 00:07:25,741
In his memoirs he wrote that the
painting was never sent back to him,

125
00:07:25,761 --> 00:07:29,840
to make certain that its last
state compensated for its first.

126
00:07:31,740 --> 00:07:35,320
Hunt and Annie made up, and
quarrelled, and made up again.

127
00:07:35,340 --> 00:07:39,708
But Annie had met someone else:
a viscount, Lord Ranelagh.

128
00:07:40,448 --> 00:07:43,813
In 1859, Hunt broke off the engagement.

129
00:07:44,683 --> 00:07:46,343
Here is the twist.

130
00:07:46,363 --> 00:07:51,383
In his painting, a kept woman hears
a song and turns towards the light.

131
00:07:51,403 --> 00:07:55,223
In his life, Hunt had cast
himself as that light.

132
00:07:55,243 --> 00:07:58,456
But Annie had her own
idea of a better life.

133
00:07:59,506 --> 00:08:02,956
Ranelagh, the story goes,
told her to sue Hunt

134
00:08:02,976 --> 00:08:05,356
for breaking his promise to marry her.

135
00:08:05,376 --> 00:08:08,076
Instead, she married his cousin.

136
00:08:08,096 --> 00:08:11,516
In July 1863, at St Pancras Church,

137
00:08:11,536 --> 00:08:14,702
Annie Miller became the wife
of Captain Thomas Thomson.

138
00:08:15,572 --> 00:08:17,472
There was one last scene.

139
00:08:17,492 --> 00:08:20,352
Annie still had a trunk
full of Hunt's letters.

140
00:08:20,372 --> 00:08:23,792
Her husband suggested threatening
to give them to the newspapers.

141
00:08:23,812 --> 00:08:26,935
Hunt's friends assumed
that he bought them back.

142
00:08:27,805 --> 00:08:31,945
Years later, Hunt saw her
again, on Richmond Hill.

143
00:08:31,965 --> 00:08:36,425
A buxom matron, as he put it,
with a carriage full of children.

144
00:08:36,445 --> 00:08:37,805
Happily married.

145
00:08:38,675 --> 00:08:44,141
Annie lived until 1925, and died
at ninety, by the sea, in Shoreham.

146
00:08:45,141 --> 00:08:47,921
So look at the woman at
the piano one more time.

147
00:08:47,941 --> 00:08:53,041
Her face is the one its owner could
live with, painted over the first.

148
00:08:53,061 --> 00:08:57,539
But her hands still tell the story:
rings on every finger but one.

149
00:08:58,539 --> 00:09:02,999
Hunt painted a woman who is saved
by a voice from outside the room.

150
00:09:03,019 --> 00:09:05,521
The real Annie found her own way out.

151
00:09:07,301 --> 00:09:10,116
It hangs in the galleries
of Tate Britain, in London.
